WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.104
"How Much Exercise Does It Take
to Boost Immunity?"

00:00:07.178 --> 00:00:10.620
Exercise can boost
our immune system by so much

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.840
that we can reduce
the number of symptom days

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.940
by 25 to 50 percent.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.880
Imagine if there was some drug
that could do that,

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.160
it would rake in billions,
whereas,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.340
exercise is free and tends
to only have good side effects.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.880
It doesn't even take much
of a workout to get results.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.640
Studies find that
if you let kids run around

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.200
for just six minutes,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.260
the levels of immune cells
circulating in their blood

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.940
can increase by 50 percent,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.560
as they pour
from the bone marrow

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.180
and redeploy
throughout the body.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:43.500
Even moderate exercise
can also boost IgA production,

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.280
protective antibodies that coat
all of our moist membranes.

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.400
Compared to
a sedentary control group,

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.800
those who performed
aerobic exercises

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.360
for thirty minutes
three times a week

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.760
for twelve weeks
had a 50 percent increase

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.100
in the levels of IgA
in their saliva

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.580
and reported significantly fewer
flu-related symptoms.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.120
Salivary IgA levels
are associated

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.460
with lower overall
mortality risk over time,

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.800
particularly cancer mortality,

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.220
perhaps as a proxy for
immune function more generally,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.160
and you can boost those levels
with exercise.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:19.860
Exercising may also boost

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.020
the activity
of natural killer cells,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.240
our immune soldiers
that focus on eliminating

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.300
both tumor cells
and virus-infected cells.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.480
Here are five different types
of cancer

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:31.800
growing in a petri dish,

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.840
mostly leukemias
and lymphomas.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.460
Draw blood from people

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.340
and drip on some
of their natural killer cells

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:38.880
into the dish,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:41.760
and they start killing off
the cancer cells.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.720
Okay, but that was blood taken
from someone before exercise.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.300
Draw blood after 30 minutes
of cycling

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.260
and drop on the same number
of natural killer cells,

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.260
and they each kill cancer
about 60 percent better.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.020
This may be one of the reasons
why exercise

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.660
seems to help
both prevent cancer

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.940
and improve cancer survival.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:06.720
Might it improve
vaccination responses?

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.960
If aerobic exercise
gets your immune cells

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.160
to spill out from your bones,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.320
can't you just slouch
on the couch all year

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.420
and just jump up
and take a brisk walk

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.100
right before getting the jab?

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.280
Studies to this effect
performed on young adults

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.320
had inconsistent results,
for example,

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.960
improving the antibody response
to a flu vaccine

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.220
in women but not men,

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.520
or to a meningitis vaccine
in men but not women.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:36.060
Against half doses
or weaker vaccines,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:39.660
acute exercise interventions
appeared to work better,

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.460
suggesting perhaps
a ceiling effect,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:45.720
whereby their immune responses
were normally so robust

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.060
exercise might not
improve them further.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.740
But maybe it would work more
consistently in older adults?

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.280
Sadly, 45-minute moderate
intensity aerobic exercise

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.500
right before flu
or pneumonia vaccinations

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.600
didn't seem to help
in older adults.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.820
Even two months

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.460
of 45-minute moderate intensity
daily activity,

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.040
six weeks before and two weeks
after vaccination,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.560
appeared insufficient
to make a difference.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.320
Eccentric resistance exercise,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.800
for example,
slowly lowering heavy dumbbells

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.800
after bicep curls
or lateral raises

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.980
to inflame the muscles right
before the vaccine goes in

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.980
also doesn't seem
to be a viable strategy.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.720
What about exercise to reduce
infection risk directly?

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.180
In a study entitled
"Moderate exercise

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:36.300
protects mice from death
due to influenzas virus,"

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.820
mice exercised
for 20 to 30 minutes

00:03:38.820 --> 00:03:40.860
four hours
after flu virus exposure

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.600
and then
each of the next three days

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.240
had nearly twice
the survival rate

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.420
(18 percent dying
compared to 57 percent dead

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:49.680
in the sedentary group).

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:53.280
There's nothing so dramatic
in the human literature,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.900
but based on a study

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.580
that followed
more than a thousand adults

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.240
through flu season,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:00.120
those who exercised
even just a few days a week

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.460
were sick
about 40 percent fewer days

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.080
than sedentary individuals.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.840
Randomized controlled
interventional trials

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:07.620
back this up.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.320
For example, one study found
that while elderly,

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.980
sedentary women randomized
to a mild range of motion

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.320
and flexibility calisthenics
control group

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.420
had a 50 percent chance
of getting

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.520
an upper-respiratory illness
during the fall season,

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.020
those randomized to begin

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.120
a half-hour-a-day
walking program

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.210
dropped their risk down
to 20 percent.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.920
For conditioned athletes,
though,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.720
the risk was just 8 percent.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.560
Exercising could potentially
make our immune systems

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:37.140
more than five times better
at fighting infection.

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.080
In a year-long study,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.560
sedentary, postmenopausal women
randomized to 45 minutes

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.020
of moderate-intensity exercise
five days per week

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.960
were experiencing
three times fewer colds

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.180
by the end of the study
compared to the control group,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.820
who were instead randomized
to weekly stretching sessions,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.560
even though the exercise group

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:57.900
only ended up exercising
around four times a week.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:00.900
A similar study
of younger overweight women

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.540
found that 15 weeks
of daily walking

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.340
cut the number of days sick

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.700
from upper respiratory tract
infections symptoms in half,

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.400
though this was due
to cutting the duration

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:12.720
of each episode in half

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.420
rather than cutting down
on the frequency of illness.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.540
In terms
of more serious infections,

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.220
randomizing older men and women

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.040
to one-hour exercise classes
twice a week for two years

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:25.260
did not, unfortunately,
cut down on the risk

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.460
of coming down with pneumonia.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.380
Although yoga training

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.360
can improve lung function
in the elderly,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.420
it has yet to be shown to help
prevent pneumonia either.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.580
There was, however,

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.920
a treatment trial
of yoga breathing exercises

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:41.400
for pulmonary tuberculosis
performed in India.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.800
Compared to
a mindfulness meditation,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.780
"breathing awareness"
control group,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.000
those randomized
to a yoga group

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.040
cleared their active infections
faster.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:53.640
Even "laughter yoga"
might help.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.260
The evolution of laughter

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.900
is thought to have been
as an antidote to stress,

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.700
the release of nervous energy.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.240
Within 60 minutes of people
watching a comedy video

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.840
(complete with "Gallagher's
classic Sledge-O-Matic finale"),

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.500
levels of the stress hormone
cortisol in their bloodstreams

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.120
were cut by more than half.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.600
Since cortisol acts
as an immunosuppressant,

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.340
which is why
cortisol-like steroids

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.480
like prednisone are used

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:20.460
for inflammatory
autoimmune diseases,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.220
the drop in cortisol
with laughter may explain

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.800
why those laughing heartily
at a humorous video

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.020
had improvements
in natural killer cell function

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.940
compared to those randomized
to a control group

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.196
watching tourism videos.

00:06:32.196 --> 00:06:34.680
(Replicating these results today

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.260
might be difficult
given the choice

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.456
of comedic stimulus:
Bill Cosby.)

